Inject a small discount policy so the checkout calculation is independent of each discount rule. This uses composition without adding a container or unnecessary class hierarchy.
```typescript
export interface DiscountPolicy {
  apply(subtotal: number): number;
}

export class PercentageDiscount implements DiscountPolicy {
  constructor(private readonly rate: number) {
    if (rate < 0 || rate > 1) {
      throw new RangeError("rate must be between 0 and 1");
    }
  }

  apply(subtotal: number): number {
    return subtotal * (1 - this.rate);
  }
}

export class Checkout {
  constructor(private readonly discount: DiscountPolicy) {}

  total(subtotal: number): number {
    if (!Number.isFinite(subtotal) || subtotal < 0) {
      throw new RangeError("subtotal must be a non-negative finite number");
    }
    return this.discount.apply(subtotal);
  }
}
```
A fixed-price policy can implement the same interface. Add tests for invalid input and each policy; use this pattern only when interchangeable discount rules are actually needed.

FastAPI validates the request model and returns the declared response status. The in-memory response below is only a placeholder; replace it with the application's persistence layer.
```python
from fastapi import FastAPI, status
from pydantic import BaseModel, Field

app = FastAPI()

class TaskCreate(BaseModel):
    title: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=160)

class TaskResponse(TaskCreate):
    id: int

@app.post("/tasks", response_model=TaskResponse,
          status_code=status.HTTP_201_CREATED)
def create_task(payload: TaskCreate) -> TaskResponse:
    return TaskResponse(id=1, title=payload.title)
```
The fixed ID is illustrative only; use a real database-generated identifier in production. Confirm the installed Pydantic version before using version-specific APIs.

Keep HTTP validation in the request DTO/pipeline and persistence in the injected service. This assumes the application has enabled Nest's validation pipe and defined the service/provider.
```typescript
import { Body, Controller, Post } from '@nestjs/common';
import { IsString, MaxLength, MinLength } from 'class-validator';
import { TasksService } from './tasks.service';

export class CreateTaskDto {
  @IsString()
  @MinLength(1)
  @MaxLength(160)
  title!: string;
}

@Controller('tasks')
export class TasksController {
  constructor(private readonly tasksService: TasksService) {}

  @Post()
  create(@Body() dto: CreateTaskDto) {
    return this.tasksService.create(dto);
  }
}
```
NestJS POST handlers return 201 by default. Configure a global `ValidationPipe` with `whitelist` and `transform` according to the project's needs, and test the controller/service contract.

The service owns the decision to send a notification, while a separate adapter owns email delivery. Depending on an interface makes the service easier to test and replace; add this abstraction only when it fits the application's needs.
```php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

interface Notifier
{
    public function send(string $recipient, string $message): void;
}

final class EmailNotifier implements Notifier
{
    public function send(string $recipient, string $message): void
    {
        // Delegate to the application's configured mailer.
    }
}

final class AlertService
{
    public function __construct(private readonly Notifier $notifier)
    {
    }

    public function alert(string $recipient, string $message): void
    {
        $this->notifier->send($recipient, $message);
    }
}
```
`AlertService` depends on the abstraction (`Notifier`), not the email implementation (dependency inversion). Delivery can change independently of alert orchestration (single responsibility); tests can inject a fake notifier.
